<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <base href="/" />
    <ResourcePreloader />
    @* The design system's webfont, fetched in parallel with the stylesheets rather than chained
       behind one by an @import. display=swap, so text is readable in the system serif from the
       first paint whatever the font does (008 research R10, NFR-001). *@
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Source+Serif+4:ital,wght@0,400;0,600;1,400&display=swap" />

    @* The vendored design system first: it defines the tokens and the base element styling every
       other sheet consumes. app.css holds what the system does not own, and the scoped bundle the
       per-component layout (008 research R2). *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["Theme/broadsheet.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["TrainingLoadAnalyzer.Web.styles.css"]" />
    <ImportMap />
    <HeadOutlet />
</head>

<body>
    @* 008: no render mode here.

       Feature 007 put InteractiveServer at the root for one reason — MudThemeProvider lived in the
       layout and needed an OnAfterRenderAsync to ask the browser for the appearance preference.
       That provider is gone and the appearance is a media query now, so the reason is gone with it
       (008 research R3). Dashboard.razor declares its own render mode, which is what the sync
       button and the window selector actually need; NotFound and Error are static, and rendering
       them over a circuit bought nothing. *@
    <Routes />
    <ReconnectModal />
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>
